DFTTest at bc5e018 (HomeOfVapourSynthEvolution/VapourSynth-DFTTest, r7 + 2:
meson's fftw3f_threads fix and a faster create loop); the same patch applies
unchanged to yuygfgg/VapourSynth-DFTTest e6f580a (bc5e018 + an sse2neon port),
which macOS arm64 builds. VapourBox task #98,
docs/reports/task-98-per-thread-buffer-maps.

The filter is fmParallel and keeps scratch buffers per worker thread in
std::unordered_map<std::thread::id, ...> members of the filter instance. Four
maps (ebuff, dftr, dftc, dftc2) are filled from getFrame and read by every
kernel with no lock at all. Two threads starting their first frame of an
instance together corrupt the map or lose an insert, and the kernel's .at()
then throws std::out_of_range inside a noexcept function: std::terminate, so
vspipe aborts ("unordered_map::at: key not found"; on Windows the UCRT's abort
is a fail-fast, exit 0xC0000409). Measured on an M1 with 8 threads: one graph
of 1000 instances aborted in 14 of 20 runs, one instance in a fresh vspipe in
5 of 200; with the patch, none in 20 and 200. Upstream issue #12 (2021)
reported this abort and was closed unreproduced. QTGMC's dfttest denoiser (and
its knlmeanscl fallback), SMDegrain's and MCTemporalDenoise's prefilters and
TemporalDegrain2 reach it.

The patch locks the map: getFrame checks for the thread's buffers under a
shared lock and allocates them under a unique lock (only the calling thread
inserts its own key), and the kernels look them up through threadBuffer(),
under the shared lock. The buffers, their reuse and so the output are
unchanged, sample for sample. It also appends "(VapourBox: locked per-thread
buffers, task #98)" to the plugin's name, which is how a test and the deps
scripts recognise a patched build. No upstream release fixes this.

Made by docs/reports/task-98-per-thread-buffer-maps/scripts/make_patches.py
and applied by download-deps-{macos,linux}.sh and download-deps-windows.ps1
right after the clone; each build fails if it stops applying.

